{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed402918419/8/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"247ef758\" lines=\"35-46\">\n<description>\nMove colored sprites from the left strip into a framed area at marker-defined targets.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background; rectangular frame on right with colored markers on its edges; sprites in a narrow left strip separated by a constant-color divider column.\n\nRule:\n- For each color, target rows/cols are where both opposite frame edges share that color. Targets are the Cartesian product.\n- Erase each sprite from the left and stamp it centered at every target. Higher sprites cover lower ones.\n\nKey insight: One sprite replicates to a grid of row × column marker intersections.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"57edb29d\" lines=\"88-103\">\n<description>\nExtract the single plain rectangle and redraw it with the aligned rectangles' border-relative marker pattern.\n\nInput structure:\n- The boundary color is the background/separator.\n- Foreground objects are dense rectangular regions; exactly one rectangle has only its base color.\n- Other rectangles contain their base color plus marker pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Crop the only plain rectangle; this crop is the output canvas.\n- Use marked rectangles whose row span overlaps the plain rectangle's row span.\n- Transfer their marker pixels by preserving each marker run's distance from the nearest rectangle edges.\n\nKey insight:\n- The marked rectangles are examples of an edge-relative pattern, so the missing plain rectangle receives the same pattern resized to its own dimensions.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d6e50e54\" lines=\"8-25\">\n<description>\nMove each maroon marker toward the blue rectangle and recolor the rectangle red.\n\nInput structure\nA background canvas contains one solid blue rectangular object and several isolated\nmaroon marker pixels. Each marker is horizontally or vertically aligned with the\nrectangle and lies outside it.\n\nRule\nStart with a background canvas, repaint the blue rectangle red, and move each\nmaroon marker straight toward the rectangle along its row or column. Exactly one\nmarker is strictly closer to the rectangle than all others and enters the\nrectangle boundary; every other marker lands in the adjacent exterior cell just\noutside its aligned side.\n\nKey insight\nThe single entering marker is the unique global nearest marker to the rectangle,\nnot the nearest marker on each of the four sides.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a6953f00\" lines=\"45-63\">\n<description>\nExtract the top-left or top-right 2x2 corner crop selected by the top edge clue.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a square color grid whose answer is one of the two 2x2 crops\n  touching the top edge and a grid corner.\n- A repeated color on the top row, when present, acts as the primary side cue.\n- Otherwise, the two top corner colors have different whole-grid frequencies.\n\nRule\n- If a top-row color repeats, find the repeated top-row occurrence nearest to\n  a left or right edge and take the 2x2 top-corner crop on that side.\n- If the top row has no repeated color, compare the whole-grid frequencies of\n  the two top-corner colors and take the side whose corner color is rarer.\n\nKey insight\n- The task is not asking for an arbitrary 2x2 search: the decisive information\n  is on the top edge, and it selects which top corner crop to keep.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5a719d11\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nWithin each horizontal pair of two-color panels, swap the motif masks and recolor each pasted mask with the source panel's base color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A separator color forms full blank rows and at least one full blank column.\n- The non-separator area is split into row bands, each containing exactly two same-shaped rectangular panels.\n- Each panel has a dominant base color and one minority motif color.\n\nRule:\n- For every row band, clear each panel to its own base color.\n- Paste the right panel's motif mask into the left panel using the right base color.\n- Paste the left panel's motif mask into the right panel using the left base color.\n\nKey insight:\n- The motif colors themselves are distractors; only each motif's shape mask and the two panel base colors are exchanged.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"898e7135\" lines=\"7-20\">\n<description>\nInflate a frame's hole-layout and fill each hole with its matching piece.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Largest component is a monochrome frame enclosing bg holes.\n- Outside pieces composed of k×k blocks; k = side of smallest solid square piece.\n\nRule:\n- Extract connected hole regions inside the frame as slots.\n- Match each piece to a slot by comparing block-level shapes under rotation/flip.\n- Output the layout scaled by k, with pieces placed in slots; frame color as background.\n\nKey insight: Pieces are k×k-block-scaled versions of holes; downsampling reveals shape matches under all 8 orientations.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f8f52ecc\" lines=\"107-122\">\n<description>\nConnect same-colored foreground components with one-cell-wide orthogonal paths around teal wall segments.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Teal cells are fixed wall segments and are never source connectors.\n- Other foreground colors appear as one or more 4-connected components; colors with a single component stay unchanged.\n\nRule\n- For each non-background, non-teal color, read its 4-connected components in row-major order.\n- Connect each consecutive pair by the shortest clear L-shaped path between the two components.\n- Clear paths may pass only through background or the same color, and they preserve the previous travel axis when both bends are possible.\n\nKey insight\n- The output is not a global recolor: each color traces a routed chain through its own components while teal cells and other colors act as blockers.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"22425bda\" lines=\"58-76\">\n<description>\nList the colors of overlaid straight lines in their only possible bottom-to-top crossing order.\n\nInput structure\nThe background is the dominant color. Every foreground color is the visible part\nof one full one-cell-thick horizontal, vertical, or 45-degree diagonal line.\nHigher lines may hide pixels of lower lines at crossings.\n\nRule\nRecover each full line from its visible collinear pixels. At any cell where\nmultiple recovered lines cross, the visible color is above every other line\nthrough that cell. Return the unique ordering of all line colors that respects\nthose above/below constraints and whose consecutive colors are lines that\ngeometrically cross.\n\nKey insight\nThe visible crossing colors give a partial layer order, and the intended answer\nis the unique Hamiltonian chain through the line-intersection graph from the\nbottom layer to the top layer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"bae5c565\" lines=\"10-28\">\n<description>\nErase the top palette row and redraw it below as a widening wedge centered on its missing cell.\n\nInput structure\n- The top row is a palette row with exactly one background gap.\n- Below it, the only foreground is one vertical marker bar of a single color.\n- The marker bar is either in the gap column or immediately beside it.\n\nRule\n- Clear the grid to background.\n- Starting at the top of the marker bar, each next row copies one more\n  column of the top-row palette on both sides of the gap.\n- Fill the gap column with the marker color, and keep the vertical marker bar.\n\nKey insight\n- The lone background cell in the top row is the center of the expanding\n  copy; the lower vertical bar provides the center color and the row where\n  the expansion begins.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3a25b0d8\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes in a target object with colors from a source object's subsprites, then tight-crop.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Two similar 8-connected objects share an outline color.\n- Source has colored subsprites in cavities; target has empty cavities.\n\nRule:\n- Find the two 8-connected objects.\n- Target = object with more hole area. Align subsprites to holes (rotation/flip if needed).\n- Fill each hole with its matched subsprite color; tight-crop.\n\nKey insight: Hole centers in the target are matched to source subsprite centers under the best rotation/flip transform.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"825aa9e9\" lines=\"111-125\">\n<description>\nDrop the moving-color objects downward until they rest above a one-row cushion before the obstacle or bottom edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has exactly three colors: empty background, a stationary obstacle/floor color, and a moving object color.\n- The obstacle color is the unique non-background role that touches the bottom edge.\n- Moving pixels start outside the obstacle cells, outside the row directly above each obstacle pixel, and outside the bottom row.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the background and obstacle pixels fixed.\n- Treat obstacle pixels, cells directly above obstacle pixels, and the bottom row as blocked.\n- Repeatedly move every 4-connected moving-color object one row down when its translated shape does not hit blocked cells or another moving object.\n\nKey insight: The visible floor has an invisible one-row cushion above it, so the objects fall under gravity but stop with one empty row before the floor or the grid bottom.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f5f939b\" lines=\"42-57\">\n<description>\nMark the missing center of each plus-shaped arrangement of four two-pixel bars with yellow.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The single foreground color forms isolated 4-connected objects, each a horizontal or vertical two-pixel bar.\n- Some bars form an implied plus sign: two horizontal bars and two vertical bars around an empty center, with a one-pixel gap between the center and each bar.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input unchanged except at implied plus-sign centers.\n- For every background pixel with foreground bars at offsets 2-3 cells left, right, above, and below, recolor that center yellow.\n- The one-pixel gaps immediately next to the center in the four cardinal directions must remain background.\n\nKey insight:\n- The yellow pixels are not extending a line; they identify the shared missing center where four separated two-pixel bars align as a plus sign.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"53fb4810\" lines=\"105-119\">\n<description>\nExtend marker tiles outward from '+' shapes by repeating them to the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- A dominant non-bg color forms several '+'-shaped objects.\n- Small colored patches (1–few cells) sit adjacent to each arm of a '+'.\n\nRule:\n- For each marker tile touching a '+', determine which side of the '+' it sits on.\n- Project the tile away from the '+' in that direction, repeating the tile pattern periodically until the grid boundary.\n- '+' pixels are never overwritten.\n\nKey insight: Each marker's attachment side determines its extension direction; the pattern repeats periodically, not as a single copy.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"c4d067a0\" lines=\"9-23\">\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
